  "query_preview": "..\nDry conditions continue across the Marianas as the seasonal doldrums\nremain over the region. A few isolated showers are seen east of Guam,\nbut these have struggled to make it on land with little to no\nrainfall seen across the island today. A trade-wind surge will move\ninto the region this weekend bringing moderate to fresh winds. As the\ntrades build, showers may increase in coverage when trade-wind\nconvergence occurs. As of now the forecast keeps isolated showers in\nthe extended range, but there will be some potential for short \nperiods of scattered showers next week. \n\nMarine...\nBenign marine conditions of 3 to 6 feet will continue until Friday\nwhen seas begin to rise due to influence from a trade-wind surge.\nCombined seas look to briefly rise to 6 to 8 feet Thursday night into\nFriday, before a short drop in seas this weekend and another rise\nheading into Monday. There will be a moderate risk for rip currents\nalong north and east facing reefs through Friday with a low risk for\nrip currents across south and west facing reefs through the week.\n\nEastern Micronesia...\nA trade-wind trough will continue to bring scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms to portions of western Pohnpei State during\nthe next 24-hours as it slowly progresses westward. Isolated to low-\nend scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to prevail at \nall three forecast points tonight and Wednesday. A trade-wind pattern\nwill develop starting on Thursday with a series of trade-wind \ntroughs expected to move across eastern Micronesia from Friday \nthrough the weekend as the ITCZ rebuilds. Wet and unsettled weather \nwith scattered to low-end numerous showers and isolated\nthunderstorms are possible across eastern Micronesia during this \ntimeframe. East to northeast winds will develop on Thursday and \nbecome moderate to fresh at all three forecast points through the \nweekend as the pressure gradient tightens. \n\nSeas of Kosrae and Pohnpei consist of a longer-period north swell \nand a small, longer-period northwest swell while the coastal waters \nof Majuro consist of the longer-period north swell and a lingering \nshorter-period east swell. Elevated surf will be possible along \nnorth-facing reefs of both Kosrae and Pohnpei through Wednesday \ngiven the longer-period north swell. Combined seas of 4 to 6 feet \nwill slowly subside through Wednesday before a northeast, trade swell\nbuilds into eastern Micronesia starting late on Friday. Combined \nseas of 4 to 7 feet can be expected by Friday and through the weekend\nat Pohnpei and Kosrae with seas reaching 8 feet at Majuro. \n\nWestern Micronesia...\nThe main weather maker is a few trade-wind troughs moving through\nChuuk state. These troughs are expected to produce scattered showers\nthroughout Chuuk State and is expected to gradually move westward \nover the next day or two. The next feature to move into this region \nis anticipated to be another section of the ITCZ with more embedded \ntroughs in the latter half of the week. This is anticipated to bring \nmore scattered showers to the region. Overall a wet week is in store \nfor Chuuk. \n\nOver in Yap and Palau, a dry pattern regime has set up and is expected\nto remain through much of the week. Showers may return near the end \nof the week as the troughs in Chuuk State make their way westward. \n\nAltimetry shows combined sea of 5 to 6 feet across the region. Winds\nare expected to be light to moderate through the weekend. Overall\nfairly benign seas are expected.",
